From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] gcc: -fapple-kext gives a virtual destructor ONE vtable slot

Against FSF GCC 15.3.0, powerpc-apple-darwin9. Applied by
darwin-cross/build-gcc.sh.

Every hardware-driving C++ IOKit kext Apple ever shipped fails to load on
this port with the same message, from the vtable patcher in libsa:

  13AppleK2Driver is not compatible with its superclass, 9IOService
  superclass changed? slot 12: superclass has
  "__ZN15OSMetaClassBase25_RESERVEDOSMetaClassBase3Ev",
  extension expects "__ZN15OSMetaClassBase25_RESERVEDOSMetaClassBase4Ev"

One slot of skew, in the first vtable the patcher was ever handed, and it is
the destructor. Apple's kext C++ ABI gives a virtual destructor ONE vtable
slot, holding the deleting destructor; the Itanium C++ ABI gives it TWO, the
complete-object destructor then the deleting one. Everything after shifts by
one and nothing matches again.

-fapple-kext exists to select Apple's shape. Measured on this toolchain
before the patch: the flag makes no difference whatsoever to vtable layout.
That is the bug. It is not a design difference and it is not xnu's fault.

What upstream kept of -fapple-kext, and what it dropped
-------------------------------------------------------

Apple's own gcc carried the kext ABI as APPLE LOCAL changes inside the C++
front end. Upstream took the option and almost none of the behaviour:

  gcc/config/darwin.opt       declares -fapple-kext -- NOT gcc/c-family/c.opt,
                              which is where it lived in Apple's tree. So it
                              is a Darwin-only option and the C++ front end
                              cannot see the variable at all, which is why
                              this patch needs a target hook rather than an
                              `if (flag_apple_kext)'.
  gcc/config/darwin.h:1229    TARGET_CXX_CDTOR_RETURNS_THIS, the one piece of
                              the ABI that did survive, and as a target hook
  gcc/config/darwin.h:1231    TARGET_KEXTABI, used in exactly two places
  gcc/config/darwin.cc:3274   force_overridable = TARGET_KEXTABI
                              && DARWIN_VTABLE_P (decl), and three lines
                              above it:  #define DARWIN_VTABLE_P(DECL) 0
                              with the comment "TODO: Add a language hook for
                              identifying if a decl is a vtable."

`grep -ri kext gcc/cp/` finds nothing. The vtable half of the ABI was never
merged, and the one surviving mention of vtables is wired to a constant 0
with a TODO. So the flag is not bit-rotted or half-merged: the destructor
behaviour was never there.

Where the decision is actually made
-----------------------------------

gcc/cp/class.cc, create_vtable_ptr(). It walks TYPE_FIELDS and gives a
vtable slot to every FUNCTION_DECL whose DECL_VINDEX is set and is not yet
an INTEGER_CST. build_cdtor_clones() has already turned one virtual
destructor into three: D2 (base), D1 (complete) and D0 (deleting).
copy_fndecl_with_name() clears DECL_VIRTUAL_P and DECL_VINDEX for the base
clone only -- class.cc:5199 -- so D1 and D0 both arrive here and both get a
slot. That is the pair, and that is the only reason there are two.

It is one slot per hierarchy, not per flag
------------------------------------------

The first cut of this patch keyed the layout off -fapple-kext alone. That is
wrong, and Apple's own binary says so. In the shipped 10.5.8 ppc kernel every
descendant of OSMetaClassBase has a single destructor slot, but
__ZTV12OSSymbolPool -- a kernel-private class in libkern/c++/OSSymbol.cpp,
virtual destructor, no relation to OSMetaClassBase, compiled in the same
translation unit with the same flags -- has TWO, D1 then D0. Read out and
symbolized:

  Apple  __ZTV12OSSymbolPool  word 2 __ZN12OSSymbolPoolD1Ev
                              word 3 __ZN12OSSymbolPoolD0Ev

So the layout is a property of the class, and the property is the
`apple_kext_compatibility' attribute -- which GCC still implements the
handler for (darwin_handle_kext_attribute), still documents as "2.95
vtable-compatibility", and xnu still puts on OSMetaClassBase, as
APPLE_KEXT_COMPATIBILITY at libkern/libkern/c++/OSMetaClass.h:46. Every
OSObject descendant inherits it; OSSymbolPool does not have it. Keying off
the attribute reproduces Apple's kernel for both, and leaves plain C++ in a
kext compiling to the standard ABI, which is what it did on Apple's
toolchain too.

What this patch does
--------------------

  gcc/target.def            new cxx target hook kext_vtable_abi_p (tree
                            type), default false. Per class, not per
                            translation unit, for the reason above.
  gcc/config/darwin.{cc,h}  Darwin's implementation: flag_apple_kext and the
                            attribute on the class or any base.
  gcc/config/darwin-protos.h  its declaration.
  gcc/doc/tm.texi{,.in}     documented, because an undocumented hook makes
                            the build regenerate tm.texi and the tree then
                            looks locally modified.
  gcc/cp/class.cc           three changes:

1. create_vtable_ptr: skip the COMPLETE-object destructor clone, so the
   surviving slot is the deleting one. Which of the two to drop is not
   arbitrary -- Apple's slot holds D0, measured.

2. finish_struct_1: the loop that assigns DECL_VINDEX walks BINFO_VIRTUALS,
   so it no longer reaches D1, which is left carrying check_for_override's
   non-INTEGER_CST marker (the decl itself, class.cc:3226, or
   error_mark_node). Anything that builds a virtual call to the complete
   destructor -- `p->~T()' on a polymorphic p -- would then index the vtable
   with that marker. So point D1's DECL_VINDEX at the surviving slot.

   This is Apple's semantics and not an approximation of them: in this ABI a
   virtual complete-object destruction deletes. darwin.cc's own comment on
   darwin_handle_kext_attribute says so out loud -- "objects CANNOT be
   allocated on the stack or as globals UNLESS they have a completely empty
   `operator delete'". A direct, non-virtual call still reaches D1 and still
   does not delete, so the module's static destructor list is unaffected,
   and build_cdtor_clones still builds all three clones, so every direct
   call is unchanged. Checked in the generated ppc: `delete p' and
   `p->~Mid()' both become one indirect branch through the single slot, and
   a static object of such a class still destructs through D1.

   Apple went further and did not emit D1 at all -- their kernel exports
   __ZN15OSMetaClassBaseD2Ev and D0Ev and no D1Ev. Ours still exports D1Ev.
   That is one extra symbol, not a layout difference, and no Apple-built
   kext can reference a symbol Apple's compiler never emitted. Keeping it is
   the deliberately smaller change: it leaves direct, non-deleting
   destruction of a stack or static object correct, which is the one thing
   Apple's ABI gave up.

3. dfs_build_vtbl_initializer: stop nulling the destructor slot of an
   abstract class under this ABI. See below.

The two zero destructor slots were not what they looked like
------------------------------------------------------------

An earlier analysis recorded that both of this port's destructor slots were
0, called it "the same class of latent hole as the CONFIG_NOKLD typo", and
asked for it to be understood before it was dismissed. It is neither
Darwin-specific nor kext-specific nor a hole in the way that note meant. It
is this, class.cc:10571:

      /* Don't refer to a virtual destructor from a constructor
         vtable or a vtable for an abstract class, since destroying
         an object under construction is undefined behavior and we
         don't want it to be considered a candidate for speculative
         devirtualization.  But do create the thunk for ABI
         compliance.  */
      if (DECL_DESTRUCTOR_P (fn_original)
          && (CLASSTYPE_PURE_VIRTUALS (DECL_CONTEXT (fn_original))
              || orig_binfo != binfo))
        init = size_zero_node;

OSMetaClassBase has pure virtuals, so it is abstract, so BOTH its destructor
slots were nulled deliberately. Reproduced on the host compiler too (g++
16.2.1, x86_64-linux, -fno-rtti): add one pure virtual to a class with a
virtual destructor and its D1/D0 entries become 0; take it away and they
come back. Nothing branches to 0 that was not already undefined behaviour,
and -fapple-kext had nothing to do with it -- it is an optimisation that
postdates gcc-4.0.1, which is why Apple's binary has a real pointer there
and ours did not.

It is still a real bug HERE, for a reason that has nothing to do with code
generation, and this is the part that analysis did not reach. libsa's
resolveKernelVTable (kld_patch.c) builds the kernel side of the comparison
by walking the kernel's own vtable from the two-word preamble and STOPPING
AT THE FIRST ZERO WORD:

    curEntry = vtableEntries + kVTablePreambleLen;
    for (classSize = 0; curEntry < endSection && *curEntry; classSize++)
        curEntry++;

A zero in the first function slot therefore gives that class a patch table
of length zero, and resolveKextVTable's comparison loop -- the loop that
produced the message at the top of this patch -- never executes. Measured in
this port's kernel before the patch: 16 of 220 kernel vtables had a zero
first function slot, so for sixteen classes, among them IOEventSource,
IOMemoryDescriptor, IODTPlatformExpert, IOCPU, IOMapper, IOUserNotification
and OSMetaClass, superclass vtable checking and patching were silently
switched off entirely. Apple's kernel has zero such tables, out of 204.
Silently skipping the check is worse than failing it, so the
abstract-class half of that condition has to go in kext mode.

The construction-vtable half (orig_binfo != binfo) is left alone: it is
about objects under construction rather than about layout, and it needs
virtual bases, which this ABI does not have.

Measured, after
---------------

__ZTV15OSMetaClassBase, read out word by word and symbolized, from Apple's
own unstripped ppc mach_kernel in MacOSXUpdCombo10.5.8.pkg (Darwin 9.8.0,
xnu-1228.15.4) and from this port's kernel built with the patched compiler:
identical, slot for slot, 16 function slots, __ZN15OSMetaClassBaseD0Ev at
word 2, __ZN15OSMetaClassBase25_RESERVEDOSMetaClassBase4Ev at function slot
12. Before the patch it was 17 function slots with _RESERVED...3 at slot 12
and two zeros at words 2 and 3.

Across the whole kernel, 204 vtables exist in both binaries under the same
name. Before: 203 of them differed in length from Apple's. After: none do.

Without -fapple-kext the generated assembly is byte-identical to the
unpatched compiler, which is the whole point of hanging it off the hook.

--- a/gcc/config/darwin.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/darwin.cc
@@ -3330,6 +3330,46 @@
   return flag_apple_kext;
 }
 
+/* True if TYPE, or any of its base classes, carries the
+   apple_kext_compatibility attribute.  */
+
+static bool
+darwin_type_has_kext_compatibility_p (tree type)
+{
+  if (!type || !RECORD_OR_UNION_TYPE_P (type))
+    return false;
+  if (lookup_attribute ("apple_kext_compatibility", TYPE_ATTRIBUTES (type)))
+    return true;
+  tree binfo = TYPE_BINFO (type);
+  if (!binfo)
+    return false;
+  tree base_binfo;
+  for (unsigned i = 0; BINFO_BASE_ITERATE (binfo, i, base_binfo); i++)
+    if (darwin_type_has_kext_compatibility_p (BINFO_TYPE (base_binfo)))
+      return true;
+  return false;
+}
+
+/* True, iff TYPE's vtable uses the kext layout: one virtual destructor slot
+   holding the deleting destructor, rather than the Itanium ABI's
+   complete/deleting pair.
+
+   -fapple-kext alone is NOT the condition, and getting that wrong is
+   measurable. In Apple's shipped 10.5.8 ppc mach_kernel every descendant of
+   OSMetaClassBase -- which is declared `} APPLE_KEXT_COMPATIBILITY;', i.e.
+   with this attribute -- has a single destructor slot, while OSSymbolPool,
+   a kernel-private class in the same translation unit with a virtual
+   destructor and no relation to OSMetaClassBase, keeps both slots. So the
+   layout is a property of the attributed hierarchy, not of the flag, which
+   is also what the attribute is documented to mean a few hundred lines
+   above: "2.95 vtable-compatibility".  */
+
+bool
+darwin_kext_vtable_abi_p (tree type)
+{
+  return flag_apple_kext && darwin_type_has_kext_compatibility_p (type);
+}
+
 void
 darwin_override_options (void)
 {
--- a/gcc/config/darwin.h
+++ b/gcc/config/darwin.h
@@ -1228,6 +1228,11 @@
 /* For Apple KEXTs, we make the constructors return this to match gcc
    2.95.  */
 #define TARGET_CXX_CDTOR_RETURNS_THIS (darwin_kextabi_p)
+/* ... and inside the apple_kext_compatibility hierarchy we give a virtual
+   destructor one vtable slot, the deleting one, rather than the Itanium
+   ABI's complete/deleting pair, again to match gcc 2.95 and every C++ kext
+   Apple ever shipped.  */
+#define TARGET_CXX_KEXT_VTABLE_ABI_P (darwin_kext_vtable_abi_p)
 #define TARGET_KEXTABI flag_apple_kext
 
 /* We have target-specific builtins.  */
